        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Parthenon</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">447 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in the building project of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>; the sculptor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phidias">Phidias</a> had general supervision and charge of its sculptural decoration; the architects Ictinos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callicrates">Callicrates</a>; marble workers, carpenters, metalworkers and labourers, slaves and foreigners working beside citizens for the same pay</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">An octastyle peripteral Doric temple of Pentelic marble, with eight columns at each end and seventeen on each side, a two-chambered cella, an Ionic frieze, 92 metopes and two sculpted pediments; its cella once held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phidias">Phidias</a>’ chryselephantine statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> Parthenos</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">Standing, much damaged and under restoration, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acropolis-of-athens">Acropolis of Athens</a>; most surviving sculptures are in the Acropolis Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, others in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and the Kunsthistorisches Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">its Doric architecture and optical refinements; the Ionic frieze, often read as the Panathenaic procession; the metopes and pediments; the lost statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> Parthenos; the explosion of 1687; the dispute over the sculptures removed by Elgin; its standing as a symbol of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, Western civilisation and democracy</span></div>
