    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Begun in 447 BCE in thanksgiving for the Greek victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, it was completed in 438 BCE, with work on its decoration continuing until 432 BCE, and also served as a treasury.</li><li>It was in turn a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, a church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> and a mosque, until a Venetian mortar round blew up the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> magazine inside it in 1687.</li><li>Agents of the 7th Earl of Elgin removed about half its surviving sculptures; in 1983 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> formally asked for the return of those in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, which has consistently refused.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Every statue on the west pediment has a fully completed back, which no one could have seen once the sculpture was in place on the temple.</p></aside>
    </section>

